[32m+[m[32m### P5 streaming anomaly detection[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mP5 runs in the intake consumer after P4 and uses River Half-Space Trees over seven bounded numeric[m
[32m+[m[32mfeatures: normalized P1 character and word counts, symbol ratio, normalized P3 cosine similarity,[m
[32m+[m[32mP3 availability, P4 malicious probability, and P4 availability. Counts are divided by the 8,000[m
[32m+[m[32mcharacter input limit; cosine similarity is mapped from `[-1, 1]` to `[0, 1]`. Missing P3/P4[m
[32m+[m[32msignals use zero plus an availability flag. HST receives fixed `[0, 1]` feature limits.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe detector uses 10 trees, height 8, window size 250, and seed 42. It scores before learning from[m
[32m+[m[32meach prompt. The first 250 observations have `p5_status = 'warming_up'` and no anomaly score; later[m
[32m+[m[32mrows have `p5_status = 'scored'`. A higher HST score indicates more unusual behavior, not a malicious[m
[32m+[m[32mverdict. P5 is informational and does not affect P7 routing.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mP3 similarity, `anomaly_score`, P5 status, feature version, and processing time are stored in[m
[32m+[m[32m`guard_results`. A database sequence preserves learning order, and the feature version makes[m
[32m+[m[32mredelivery idempotent. On startup the detector restores its recent two HST windows from stored[m
[32m+[m[32mP1/P3/P4 inputs, including the window offset. No Kafka topic is added.[m
